{
 "slug":"how-to-release-a-song-in-india","cat":"Releasing",
 "title":"How to release a song in India: a step-by-step guide",
 "desc":"A plain, step-by-step guide to releasing your first single in India, from mastering and artwork to distribution, ISRC, release date, and what to do on drop day. No label needed.",
 "lead":"You do not need a label or a manager to put a song on Spotify, Apple Music and JioSaavn. You need a clear checklist. Here it is.",
 "tldr":"To release a song in India: finish and master the track, prepare square artwork and metadata, pick a <a href=\"/guides/best-music-distributor-india/\">distributor</a>, set a release date at least two to four weeks out so you can pitch, get your <a href=\"/guides/isrc-code-india/\">ISRC</a> and register your composition with IPRS, then plan a simple drop-day. The whole thing costs less than a dinner out if you choose the right distributor.",
 "sections":[
   {"h2":"Step 1: Finish and master the track","body":[
     "Do not upload a rough mix. Get the song mixed and mastered to a competitive loudness, streaming platforms normalise volume, but a muddy master still loses listeners in the first ten seconds. If budget is tight, an affordable online mastering service beats an unmastered upload.",
     "Export a high-quality WAV (16-bit/44.1kHz minimum). Keep the session files, you will want stems later for sync, remixes and reels."]},
   {"h2":"Step 2: Artwork and metadata","body":[
     "Artwork must be square (3000x3000px), with no logos, URLs or promotional text, distributors reject those. Get the details right, they are permanent once live:",
     {"ul":["<b>Primary artist name</b>, spelled exactly how you want it everywhere.",
            "<b>Track title</b> and any features listed correctly.",
            "<b>Songwriter and producer credits</b>, you need these for royalties.",
            "<b>Genre and language</b>, which affect where you get placed."]}]},
   {"h2":"Step 3: Choose a distributor","body":[
     "A distributor delivers your track to every platform and collects your money. The choice is mostly flat annual fee versus a revenue cut, run the math on your real numbers. Our full breakdown is in <a href=\"/guides/best-music-distributor-india/\">the best music distributor for India</a>, and the platforms Indian listeners actually use are in <a href=\"/guides/music-platforms-independent-artists-india/\">music platforms for independent artists</a>.",
     "One rule above all: a distributor can take a fee or a share, never your rights. Read the agreement for any ownership-transfer language."]},
   {"h2":"Step 4: Set a release date two to four weeks out","body":[
     "Do not release the day you upload. Set the date at least two to four weeks ahead. That window is what lets you pitch Spotify editorial through Spotify for Artists and reach out to <a href=\"/reviewers/\">blogs, playlists and curators</a> before the song is live, which is when it counts.",
     "Claim your Spotify for Artists and Apple Music for Artists profiles in that window so the release lands on a real, verified profile."]},
   {"h2":"Step 5: ISRC, copyright and publishing","body":[
     "Your distributor usually assigns an <a href=\"/guides/isrc-code-india/\">ISRC</a> automatically, that is the fingerprint tracking your recording's plays. Separately, register your composition (the lyrics and melody) with IPRS so you collect publishing royalties, distributors do not do this for you. Details in <a href=\"/guides/register-music-copyright-iprs-india/\">registering music copyright and IPRS in India</a>."]},
   {"h2":"Step 6: Drop day and after","body":[
     "On release day: share the link everywhere, add it to your Instagram bio and pinned story, and ask your close network to save the track (saves matter more than a one-time play). Then keep going, one release rarely moves the needle. Sequencing releases so each builds on the last is the difference between growth and disappearing, which is the heart of the <a href=\"/guides/12-week-release-plan-india/\">12-week release plan</a>."]}],
 "faq":[
   ("How much does it cost to release a song in India?","With a flat-fee distributor you can release for roughly the price of a small annual subscription, often under a couple of thousand rupees a year for unlimited songs. Mixing and mastering are the bigger variable cost."),
   ("Do I need a label to release music in India?","No. Any independent artist can release directly through a distributor. A label is one option, not a requirement, and most take rights in exchange."),
   ("How long before release should I upload?","Upload and schedule at least two to four weeks ahead so you can pitch Spotify editorial and reach curators while the song is still unreleased.")]
},
{
 "slug":"isrc-code-india","cat":"Rights",
 "title":"How to get an ISRC code in India (free, step by step)",
 "desc":"What an ISRC code is, why every Indian artist needs one, and how to get an ISRC in India for free through your distributor, or directly from IFPI, without paying a middleman.",
 "lead":"An ISRC is the barcode of your recording. Here is what it does and how to get one in India without overpaying.",
 "tldr":"An <b>ISRC</b> (International Standard Recording Code) uniquely identifies your specific recording so every stream, sale and radio play is counted and paid to you. In India, the easiest way to get one is free: your <a href=\"/guides/best-music-distributor-india/\">distributor</a> assigns an ISRC automatically when you upload. If you want to issue your own, you register as a manager with IFPI's local agency. Never pay a reseller a per-code fee you do not need to.",
 "sections":[
   {"h2":"What an ISRC actually is","body":[
     "An ISRC is a 12-character code (like IN-XY7-26-00001) attached to one recording. It follows that recording everywhere, so if you change distributor later, keeping the same ISRC means your stream counts and playlist adds carry over. A different mix or a remaster gets its own ISRC; the same master keeps one for life."]},
   {"h2":"The easy way: let your distributor assign it (free)","body":[
     "For almost every independent artist this is the answer. When you upload a track, your distributor issues an ISRC at no extra cost. You do not need to apply anywhere. Check your release dashboard, the ISRC appears there, and note it down for your records."]},
   {"h2":"The direct way: become your own ISRC manager","body":[
     "If you release at scale or want to control your own codes, you can register as an ISRC manager and be issued a registrant prefix, then generate your own ISRCs for every recording. In India this goes through IFPI's appointed national agency. This makes sense for labels and prolific artists, not for a first single.",
     "Whatever route you take, keep a simple spreadsheet of every track and its ISRC. It is the backbone of getting paid correctly."]},
   {"h2":"Do not pay a reseller","body":[
     "Some sites sell ISRCs individually at inflated prices. You rarely need this: your distributor's free codes are valid and permanent. Paying per code only makes sense in specific label workflows.",
     "ISRC is for your recording. It is separate from your <a href=\"/guides/register-music-copyright-iprs-india/\">composition rights and IPRS registration</a>, which cover the song itself. You need both handled."]}],
 "faq":[
   ("Is an ISRC free in India?","Yes, through a distributor it is free, they assign one automatically when you upload. You only pay if you choose to become your own registrant or buy from a reseller, which most artists do not need."),
   ("Do I need a new ISRC for every song?","Yes, every distinct recording gets its own ISRC. A remix or a re-recording is a new recording and needs a new code; the same master keeps the same code forever."),
   ("Is an ISRC the same as copyright?","No. An ISRC identifies the recording for tracking and payment. Copyright and IPRS registration protect and monetise the underlying composition. They are two separate things you both need.")]
},
{
 "slug":"free-music-distribution-india","cat":"Distribution",
 "title":"Free music distribution in India: is it actually worth it?",
 "desc":"Free and cheap music distribution options in India compared honestly, what free distributors take in return, when free is fine, and when a flat fee keeps more money in your pocket.",
 "lead":"Free distribution is real, but nothing is truly free. Here is what you actually give up, and when that trade is worth it.",
 "tldr":"Several distributors let you get on Spotify, Apple Music and JioSaavn for free, they make money by taking a <b>percentage of your royalties</b> (often around 10 to 20%) instead of an upfront fee. Free is fine when your streams are low or uncertain. Once you earn steadily, a <b>flat annual fee</b> usually keeps more money in your pocket. Do the math at your real numbers, not the marketing.",
 "sections":[
   {"h2":"How free distribution makes money","body":[
     "A free distributor charges nothing upfront and instead keeps a cut of everything you earn, forever, or until you leave. That is not a scam, it is a revenue-split model. The question is only whether the split costs you more than a flat fee would at your streaming volume."]},
   {"h2":"When free is the right choice","body":[
     {"ul":["You are releasing your first songs and have no idea what they will earn.",
            "Your monthly streams are low, so a percentage of a small number is a small number.",
            "You want zero financial risk to get started."]},
     "For most new artists, starting free is completely sensible. Use our <a href=\"/tools/royalty-calculator/\">streaming royalty calculator</a> to see roughly what your streams are worth first."]},
   {"h2":"When a flat fee wins","body":[
     "Once a song earns consistently, a percentage cut adds up fast. Run the comparison: if 15% of your annual royalties is more than a flat-fee distributor's yearly price, switch. Frequent releasers almost always come out ahead on flat fee. The full model comparison is in <a href=\"/guides/best-music-distributor-india/\">the best music distributor for India</a>."]},
   {"h2":"The non-negotiables, free or paid","body":[
     {"ul":["<b>Your rights stay yours.</b> Free or paid, the distributor takes money, never ownership of your master or publishing.",
            "<b>Clean exit.</b> Check you can leave and keep your <a href=\"/guides/isrc-code-india/\">ISRCs</a> so your streams carry over.",
            "<b>India reach.</b> Confirm delivery to JioSaavn and Gaana, and caller-tune support if that channel matters."]}]}],
 "faq":[
   ("What is the catch with free music distribution?","The catch is a percentage cut of your royalties instead of an upfront fee. It is a fair trade at low volumes, but it can cost more than a flat fee once you earn steadily."),
   ("Can I switch from a free distributor to a paid one later?","Yes, if you migrate carefully and keep the same ISRCs so playlists and stream counts carry over. Never take music down without a migration plan."),
   ("Which is cheaper, free or flat-fee distribution?","Whichever is cheaper at your actual streaming numbers. Low or uncertain streams favour free; steady, higher earnings favour a flat annual fee.")]
},
{
 "slug":"submit-music-spotify-playlists-india","cat":"Playlists",
 "title":"How to submit music to Spotify playlists in India",
 "desc":"How to pitch Spotify editorial playlists through Spotify for Artists, how to reach independent and curator playlists in India, and how to avoid the pay-for-play playlist scams.",
 "lead":"There are two real doors to Spotify playlists, and one trap. Here is how to work all three.",
 "tldr":"To get on Spotify playlists in India: first, pitch <b>Spotify editorial</b> through Spotify for Artists at least a week before release (this also helps Release Radar and Discover Weekly). Second, pitch <b>independent and curator playlists</b> directly, our <a href=\"/reviewers/\">reviewer and curator database</a> lists real ones. Avoid anyone guaranteeing placements for a fee, bot playlists get your track flagged, not heard.",
 "sections":[
   {"h2":"Door 1: Spotify editorial (free, the big one)","body":[
     "In Spotify for Artists, use the pitch tool on any unreleased track. You can pitch one song at a time, ideally seven or more days before release. Even if you do not land an editorial playlist, pitching signals the release to Spotify's algorithm and feeds Release Radar and Discover Weekly for your existing listeners.",
     "Write the pitch like a human: genre, mood, instruments, the story, and any momentum you have. Specific beats generic."]},
   {"h2":"Door 2: independent and curator playlists","body":[
     "Beyond Spotify's own editors, thousands of independent curators run playlists. Reaching the right ones is a numbers-and-fit game. Our <a href=\"/reviewers/\">database of 1,100+ reviewers, curators and playlists</a> is filterable by genre, cost and whether they focus on South Asian music, so you pitch fit, not spray.",
     "Personalise every pitch. Name the playlist, say why your song fits it, keep it short, and include the Spotify link and a one-line bio."]},
   {"h2":"The trap: pay-for-play playlists","body":[
     "Anyone guaranteeing a fixed number of streams or a placement for cash is selling bot or fake-engagement playlists. Spotify detects artificial streams and can remove your track or withhold royalties. It never converts to real fans. Do not do it."]},
   {"h2":"What actually makes playlists say yes","body":[
     {"ul":["A finished, well-mastered track that fits the playlist's sound.",
            "Some existing momentum, saves, a growing profile, a story.",
            "A clean, verified Spotify for Artists profile.",
            "A pitch that proves you actually listened to their playlist."]},
     "Editorial and curator support compounds with everything else you do. Pair it with the <a href=\"/guides/spotify-editorial-playlists-india/\">editorial playlist guide</a> and a real <a href=\"/guides/12-week-release-plan-india/\">release plan</a>."]}],
 "faq":[
   ("How do I pitch Spotify editorial playlists in India?","Use the pitch tool in Spotify for Artists on an unreleased track, at least seven days before release. Describe the genre, mood and story clearly. It also helps your algorithmic playlists even if editorial passes."),
   ("Should I pay for Spotify playlist placement?","No. Paid-placement and guaranteed-stream playlists use bots that Spotify detects and penalises. It risks your track and never builds real fans."),
   ("How do I find genuine Spotify curators in India?","Use a vetted, filterable database rather than spraying DMs. Our reviewer database lists real curators, including ones focused on South Asian music, with cost and genre filters.")]
},
{
 "slug":"how-much-indian-artists-earn-spotify","cat":"Money",
 "title":"How much do Indian artists earn from Spotify?",
 "desc":"A realistic look at how much Indian artists earn from Spotify and streaming, per-stream rates in India versus internationally, why India pays less, and how to estimate your own income.",
 "lead":"The honest numbers on streaming income in India, and why the per-stream rate here is lower than the figures you read online.",
 "tldr":"There is no fixed per-stream rate, but in India streaming typically pays roughly <b>a few paise to around ten paise per stream</b>, so about ₹10 to ₹30 for 1,000 streams, lower than the often-quoted international figures because Indian ad and subscription revenue per user is lower. International streams of your music pay far more. Estimate your own with our <a href=\"/tools/royalty-calculator/\">streaming royalty calculator</a>.",
 "sections":[
   {"h2":"Why India pays less per stream","body":[
     "Streaming royalties are not a fixed rate; they are a share of the platform's revenue in your listener's country. India has huge listenership but low average revenue per user, cheaper subscriptions and lower ad rates, so the per-stream payout for Indian streams is smaller than in the US or Europe. Ten thousand streams from Indian listeners and ten thousand from the US pay very differently."]},
   {"h2":"A realistic range","body":[
     "Treat any single number with suspicion, but as a working range, Indian streams often land somewhere around ₹0.03 to ₹0.10 each depending on the platform and listener mix. Apple Music tends to pay more per stream than Spotify; YouTube's ad-supported plays pay the least. So 100,000 streams that are mostly Indian might return a few thousand rupees, not lakhs.",
     "Run your own scenario in the <a href=\"/tools/royalty-calculator/\">royalty calculator</a>, adjust the India-versus-international split and watch the number move. That split is the biggest lever on your income."]},
   {"h2":"How to actually earn more","body":[
     {"ul":["<b>Grow international listeners.</b> Even a small share of US, UK or European streams lifts your average payout sharply.",
            "<b>Keep 100% of your rights</b> so you are not splitting the little there is. See <a href=\"/guides/best-music-distributor-india/\">choosing a distributor</a>.",
            "<b>Collect your publishing.</b> Register with IPRS so you get composition royalties on top of recording royalties. See <a href=\"/guides/register-music-copyright-iprs-india/\">IPRS registration</a>.",
            "<b>Do not rely on streaming alone.</b> Live shows, sync and direct fan support usually out-earn streams for independent artists. See <a href=\"/guides/make-money-independent-artist-india/\">making money as an independent artist</a>."]}]},
   {"h2":"The bigger point","body":[
     "Streaming is a discovery and credibility layer, not a salary. The artists who earn treat streams as the top of a funnel that leads to gigs, sync and a real fanbase, not the destination. That is the whole approach behind our <a href=\"/programme/\">programme</a>."]}],
 "faq":[
   ("How much does Spotify pay per stream in India?","There is no fixed rate, but Indian streams often pay roughly ₹0.03 to ₹0.10 each, so about ₹10 to ₹30 per 1,000 streams. International streams of your music pay considerably more."),
   ("How many streams to earn ₹1,00,000 on Spotify in India?","At Indian per-stream rates it can take a few million streams to reach ₹1,00,000 from Spotify alone, which is why serious independent income also comes from live shows, sync and publishing."),
   ("Does Apple Music pay more than Spotify in India?","Generally yes, Apple Music tends to pay a higher per-stream rate than Spotify, while YouTube's ad-supported streams pay the least. Your India-versus-international listener split matters more than the platform.")]
},
